  "content": {
    "title": "Austin FC vs Toronto FC score: 3 takeaways from MLS draw",
    "text": "TORONTO, CANADA - APRIL 18: Jon Bell #15 of the Austin FC celebrates his goal against the Toronto FC during the first half of their MLS soccer match at BMO Field on April 18, 2026 in Toronto, Ontario, Canada. (Photo by Mark Blinch/Getty Images)Mark Blinch/Getty ImagesBack on track? Maybe. But following two subpar showings, Austin FC looked like a completely different side Saturday during a 3-3 road draw with Toronto FC at BMO Field. With far and away their best performance of the season in the attack, the Verde & Black were terrific in possession and the buildup on Facundo Torres’ goal was worthy of play in some of the better European leagues. Jon Bell and Christian Ramírez also scored while Daniel Salloi, Richie Laryea and Kobe Franklin found the net for Toronto. Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢Here are some key takeaways as Austin FC (1-3-4, 7 points) has another quick turnaround and long flight ahead, playing Wednesday at San Jose. Austin FC's attack gives it a well-earned pointToronto hasn’t lost a home match in 12 months, so a draw here — as also noted in the American-Statesman’s game advance — is a good result for the club. Article continues below this adSure, at some point in the season it’s going to need to start compiling wins. But without Owen Wolff, Dani Pereira and Brandon Vazquez, all of whom could be playing again in May, and after a midweek match, this is an outcome the club takes at any point. (Club brass wouldn’t say that on the record, but one would guess behind closed doors there’s a different feeling.)The game as a whole showed Austin FC’s potential on offense when it gets good performances from its top attacking players. Torres and Joseph Rosales were excellent and Uzuni and Jayden Nelson were very good. Rosales, who normally plays on the wing, played more in the central midfield and thrived. Austin FC coach Nico Estévez said Thursday the club needed to be more aggressive and ruthless with its possession, and after two games where the club looked like it had no idea of what it wanted to do in the attack, that’s exactly what he received. Article continues below this adKudos also has to be given to Estévez for fielding the most optimal attacking lineup in a match where the club needed a result. Given the club’s injuries, his forward and midfield selections put the best talent available on the field.TORONTO, CANADA - APRIL 18: Josh Sargent #9 of the Toronto FC goes up for a header against Oleksandr Svatok #5 of the Austin FC during the first half of their MLS soccer match at BMO Field on April 18, 2026 in Toronto, Ontario, Canada. (Photo by Mark Blinch/Getty Images)Mark Blinch/Getty ImagesTaylor spurs brilliant goal There can’t be enough praise for the quality of passing between Robert Taylor, Torres and Myrto Uzuni that led to Torres’ goal.Article continues below this adAnd though Torres scored, Taylor should get the bulk of the credit. He started the sequence with a long pass from the wing to Torres in the middle of the field. Instead of jogging, he then sprinted down the field, blowing past a Toronto defender, and laid off Uzuni’s cross perfectly to Torres in the middle of the box. Taylor has underwhelmed since joining the club, but he provided a spark off the bench in the second half.Luck of the bounceAustin FC might have been a bit unlucky defensively. Toronto’s first two goals came after deflections on Verde & Black defenders and its third followed a bounce off the post. Article continues below this adThat’s soccer, though. The Verde & Black also benefited from some deflections as Bell’s goal went off a Toronto defender and Ramírez found the ball perfectly at his feet on his feet after a cross ricocheted off a defender. Bottom lineIt was a proper response by Austin FC after two rough showings. With seven matches before the World Cup break, the Verde & Black need to tread water in the Western Conference to give themselves a shot in the second half of the season. Article continues below this adAnd if they can consistently produce performances like Saturday, they should do just that. Get the hookup and your Horns Up with Longhorns coverage.April 18, 2026Austin FC and high school sports reporterColby Gordon has written for the Austin American-Statesman since 2018. Covering Austin FC since late in its inaugural season and a member of the preps beat with Rick Cantu, his previous work has appeared in the Kansas City Star, Dallas Morning News, Toronto Star and Independence Examiner, just to name a few. Originally from the Midwest, he earned degrees in mass communications and education prior to beginning his newspaper career.",
